  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Friday...\n\nScattered to widespread showers and weak thunderstorms will \ncontinue into the late afternoon and early evening, as a shortwave\nmoves through the Northern Rockies from NW to SE. Weak \ninstability and shear values across southern MT and far northern \nWY will limit storm and shower strength today. The primary \nconcerns are brief downpours, small hail/graupel, and erratic wind\ngusts up to 40 mph. Cold air funnels are possible today, with one\nbeing reported this morning near the Pryor mountains. These \nfunnels are unlikely to reach the ground but if you see any please\nreport them, especially if you do see them approaching the \nground.\n\nForecast precipitation amounts are light to moderate, a few \nhundredths of an inch to a few tenths. The best chance for at \nleast 0.1\" of precip in the lower elevations will exist between \nHarlowton to Sheridan and east from Forsyth and Broadus. \nPrecipitation will diminish through the evening hours, though an \nadditional wave is depicted by models bringing a low chance of \nshowers from Roundup to Baker overnight.\n\nBeginning Sunday, ridging will generally prevail into midweek, \nbringing above to well above normal temperatures and dry \nconditions. An upper wave moves across northern MT on Monday along\nwith a surface low in southern Canada. The combination of these \nwill bring breezy conditions to much of southern MT, with winds \ngusting in the teens to low 30s mph. For locations north and east \nof Billings, the extent of any precipitation today will need to be\nmonitored as increased fire concerns are possible with the warmer\nand drier conditions approaching. For now, continue to monitor \nthe latest forecast for updates and take care not to cause a spark\nas we begin the new week.\n\nThe second half of the week looks to become unsettled, with \ndisturbances moving through the ridge. Ensembles are not in full \nagreement on the pattern though their current depictions generally\nfavor shower and thunderstorm development.\n\nLook for high temperatures in the 70s to low 80s on Sunday, \nwarming into the 80s and potentially low 90s by Wednesday. The \nsecond half of the week has highs forecast back in the 70s and \n80s.\n\nMatos",
